% Lexical structure: comments, literals, escapes, interpolation, identifiers.

### name: lex.comment
--- source
1 + 1  # this is ignored
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: lex.comment.hash-in-text
--- source
"a#b"
--- expect
text "a#b"
===
### name: lex.ident.case-insensitive
--- setup
Total = 5
--- source
TOTAL + total
--- expect
num 10
===
### name: lex.escapes
--- source
"a\\b\"c\nd\te\rf"
--- expect
text "a\\b\"c\nd\te\rf"
===
### name: lex.escape.brace
--- source
"\{not interpolated\}"
--- expect
text "{not interpolated}"
===
### name: lex.escape.codepoint
--- source
"\u{17C}\u{1F44D}"
--- expect
text "ż👍"
===
### name: lex.escape.unknown
--- source
"a\qb"
--- expect
error E_ESCAPE
===
### name: lex.escape.surrogate-rejected
--- source
"\u{D800}"
--- expect
error E_RANGE
===
### name: lex.raw.no-escapes
--- source
'a\db\\c'
--- expect
text "a\\db\\\\c"
===
### name: lex.raw.doubled-quote
--- source
'it''s'
--- expect
text "it's"
===
### name: lex.raw.no-interpolation
--- source
'{A}'
--- expect
text "{A}"
===
### name: lex.text.unterminated
--- source
"abc
--- expect
error E_UNTERMINATED
===

% Interpolation lowers to `&` and keeps empty segments, so it always goes
% through the checks in §5.2.

### name: lex.interp.basic
--- setup
A = 3
--- source
"value {A} here"
--- expect
text "value 3 here"
===
### name: lex.interp.expression
--- setup
A = 3
--- source
"{A + 1}"
--- expect
text "4"
===
### name: lex.interp.nested-text
--- setup
A = 2
--- source
"x{ IF(A > 1, "big", "small") }y"
--- expect
text "xbigy"
===
### name: lex.interp.brace-inside-nested-text
--- source
"{ "a}b" }"
--- expect
text "a}b"
===
### name: lex.interp.applies-scalar-context
--- setup
A = (7, 8)
--- source
"{A}"
--- expect
text "7"
===
### name: lex.interp.rejects-bool
--- source
"{1 == 1}"
--- expect
error E_NOT_TEXT
===
### name: lex.interp.empty
--- source
"a{}b"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX
===
### name: lex.interp.unterminated
--- source
"a{1+1"
--- expect
error E_UNTERMINATED
===
### name: lex.number.no-trailing-dot
--- source
1.
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX
===
### name: lex.unknown-character
--- source
1 @ 2
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:3
===
### name: lex.escape.unicode-trailing-newline
--- note
The hex digits of a \u{...} escape are ASCII and nothing else, a newline
included. Same accident as num.not-a-number.trailing-newline: an anchored hex
pattern matched as a prefix accepts a trailing newline in Python and PCRE.
--- source
"\u{41\n}"
--- expect
error E_ESCAPE
===
### name: lex.space.bom-is-not-whitespace
--- note
A byte-order mark is not whitespace in SEL and is not skipped, so a source
file saved by an editor that writes one does not parse. That is a decision --
silently skipping it would make two byte-different files the same program --
and until now it was only a decision, asserted nowhere. The POSITION is the
assertion: 1:1 says the BOM was seen and refused, where a host that stripped
it transparently would answer TRUE and pass any test that only checked that.
--- source
﻿1 == 1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:1
===
### name: lex.space.crlf-between-tokens
--- note
CR is whitespace, so a file with Windows line endings is the same program as
one without. Five hosts agree; nothing said so.
--- source
1 ==
1
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: lex.space.lone-cr
--- note
And a bare CR, which is not a line ending on any system still in use but is
what a truncated CRLF leaves behind.
--- source
1 ==1
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: lex.space.crlf-inside-a-text-literal
--- note
A CRLF inside a text literal is two characters of the text. The case is also
a check on the runner: a .selt reader that opens the file with newline
translation (Python's universal newlines, a line API that strips the CR
before LF) hands the lexer "a\nb" and gets 3 -- the two lex.space CR cases
above pass either way, because there the CR is whitespace.
--- source
LEN("a
b")
--- expect
num 4
===
### name: lex.space.lone-cr-inside-a-text-literal-is-not-lf
--- note
A bare CR inside a literal stays a CR; a reader that translates it to LF
makes the two texts equal.
--- source
"ab" $== "a
b"
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: lex.space.unicode-spaces-are-not-whitespace
--- note
NBSP is the one a user actually produces, by copying an expression out of a
web page or a word processor. It looks exactly like a space and is not one.
Refusing it is right -- guessing which of Unicode Zs to accept is how a
grammar stops being a grammar -- but the error has to be the one that says
where, so a reader can see the character they cannot see.
--- source
1 == 1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===
### name: lex.space.next-line-is-not-whitespace
--- source
1 ==1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===
### name: lex.space.vertical-tab-is-not-whitespace
--- note
VT and FF are whitespace to C, to most regex engines, and to several host
languages. SEL takes space, tab, CR and LF and nothing else, so a host that
reached for its own isspace() would diverge here and only here.
--- source
1 ==1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===
### name: lex.space.form-feed-is-not-whitespace
--- source
1 ==1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===

### name: lex.interp.unbalanced-close
--- note
An interpolation body is its own unit: its parentheses must balance inside the
braces (SPEC §2). Spliced raw, `"{1) + (2}"` closed the parenthesis the lexer
opens around the body and reopened one of its own, and evaluated to 3. The
error is at the stray closer.
--- source
"{1) + (2}"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:4
===

### name: lex.interp.unbalanced-close-in-call
--- note
The same splice inside an argument list turned one argument into two:
`COUNT("{1), (2}")` was 2.
--- source
COUNT("{1), (2}")
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:10
===

### name: lex.interp.unbalanced-close-discards-text
--- note
Before the rule the literal text around the body was silently dropped as well:
`"a{1);(2}"` was 2.
--- source
"a{1);(2}"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===

### name: lex.interp.unbalanced-open
--- note
An opener still open at the closing brace is `E_SYNTAX` at that brace. It used
to be an error only by accident, at the end of the input, because the missing
parenthesis was found there.
--- source
"{(1}"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===

### name: lex.interp.unbalanced-bracket-mismatch
--- note
Brackets balance separately from parentheses: a `]` cannot close a `(`.
--- source
"{(1]}"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===

### name: lex.interp.unbalanced-inner-first
--- note
A nested literal's body is checked when that literal is lexed, before the
enclosing body has finished, so the innermost error is the one reported.
--- source
"{ (1 + "x{(}") }"
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:13
===

### name: lex.interp.balanced-nested
--- note
The control: balanced bodies, including a nested literal with its own
parentheses inside a parenthesised body, are unaffected.
--- source
"{(1) + "{(2)}"}"
--- expect
text "3"
===

### name: lex.interp.balanced-with-index
--- note
Brackets inside a body balance like parentheses.
--- source
A = (10, 20); "{A[2]}"
--- expect
text "20"
===
